lesson(2,"Partnerships","explain shared ownership and calculate a partner’s agreed profit share",
 "the sole proprietor’s finance and control limits","companies and the distinction between owners’ liability and business debts",
 "A partnership brings co-owners together to conduct a business and share its outcomes under an agreement. Partners may combine funds, skills and work, but must coordinate decisions. A partnership agreement can specify contributions, duties, profit sharing and handling disputes or departures. This introductory comparison uses an ordinary general partnership; limited-partnership forms differ. In that ordinary model, personal liability may be extensive. Do not assume that profit is shared equally or only according to capital unless the agreement says so.",
 "Bih supplies equipment and Ayuk provides funds to an imaginary Bamenda workshop. They agree on duties and a profit-sharing ratio. A disagreement about opening hours reveals a coordination cost. Their classroom agreement is an economic illustration, not a legally sufficient document or advice about the correct OHADA form.",
 "Read the agreement in the question. Separate capital contributions from profit allocations. Add ratio parts before dividing the distributable total, and check the partners’ shares sum to that total. Explain both the benefit of pooled resources and the possibility of conflict.",[
 F("shared ownership","co-owners jointly holding interests in the business","Bih and Ayuk own the workshop together.","Every person paid by a partnership becomes a partner.","Co-ownership is different from being an employee or supplier."),
 F("partnership agreement","the arrangement stating the partners’ rights, contributions and responsibilities","The model specifies who supplies equipment and who keeps records.","All partnerships must share profits equally regardless of their agreement.","The exercise’s agreed allocation determines its shares."),
 F("pooled expertise","combining different partners’ relevant skills","A repair specialist works with a partner skilled in record-keeping.","Adding partners removes every skill gap automatically.","Complementary skills can help, but their actual relevance and coordination matter."),
 F("decision conflict","disagreement between co-owners about business choices","The partners disagree about opening hours.","Shared ownership always eliminates disagreement.","Different priorities can make decisions slower or require agreed procedures.")],share,prereq=[1])

capital=number(3,"shares","A fictional company issues {n} shares with nominal value {v} CFA francs per share. What is the nominal issued share capital?","n*v",{"n":{"int":[20,90]},"v":{"pick":[100,200,500]}},
 ["Nominal issued share capital = shares issued × nominal value.","{n} × {v} = {answer} CFA francs."],[("add","n+v","Adding a share count to a currency value does not give capital."),("one","v","That is the value per share, not the value of all issued shares.")])
lesson(3,"Joint-stock and limited-liability companies: ownership","distinguish a company from its owners and calculate nominal issued share capital",
 "ownership and liability in sole proprietorships and partnerships","company governance and the mix of equity and borrowing",
 "An incorporated company has a legal identity distinct from its owners. In the limited-liability company forms studied, owners’ ordinary responsibility for company debts is generally limited to their agreed contributions, subject to applicable rules and separate obligations such as guarantees. Shares represent ownership interests; the company’s property is not each shareholder’s personal property. Continuity can extend beyond a particular owner’s departure. A company may have a single owner where the legal form allows it, so counting owners alone does not establish the form.",
 "An imaginary Douala processing company raises equity by issuing shares. A shareholder owns an interest, not a right to take a company vehicle home. The business may continue when an owner sells an interest under the applicable rules. All values in this exercise are nominal classroom amounts, not quoted market prices or legal minimum capital.",
 "Separate company, shareholder and manager. Multiply the issued share count by nominal value to find nominal issued capital. Keep nominal value distinct from market price and the company’s total asset value. Limited liability is not a promise that a shareholder cannot lose an investment.",[
 F("separate legal identity","the company being legally distinct from its owners","A shareholder cannot treat the company’s vehicle as personal property.","A shareholder’s ownership interest makes every company asset their personal possession.","Company assets belong to the company, not individually to each shareholder."),
 F("limited liability","owners’ ordinary debt exposure being limited as the legal form provides","An investor’s agreed contribution is distinguished from the company’s total debt.","Limited liability guarantees that an investment cannot lose value.","It limits specified exposure, not investment losses or separate guarantees."),
 F("share","an ownership interest in a company","The model issues ownership interests to contributors.","A share is always a loan repayable on a fixed maturity date.","Ownership finance differs from a debt claim."),
 F("continuity","the company’s ability to continue beyond a particular owner’s departure","The company remains while an ownership interest changes hands under its rules.","A company must always close whenever one shareholder leaves.","The distinct company can continue despite ownership changes.")],capital,
 worked({},"Find nominal issued capital for {40} shares of {500} CFA francs.",["Use the number actually issued, not an unused authorisation.","{40} × {500} = {40*500}."],"{40*500} CFA francs."),prereq=[2])

