lesson(8,"Cooperatives: purpose, ownership and control","explain common member needs and distinguish democratic control from capital-based voting",
 "comparison of ownership, finance and control","types of cooperative and the use of a cooperative surplus",
 "A cooperative is a voluntarily formed, jointly owned and democratically controlled enterprise meeting members’ common needs. Primary cooperatives normally follow one member, one vote under the cooperative principle. Capital is needed, but a larger contribution does not by itself create proportionately more votes in that model. Membership involves responsibilities as well as benefits. A cooperative can earn a surplus; it is not defined as an organisation forbidden to trade or charge prices. Exact form and governance rules must be checked under the relevant framework.",
 "Fictional growers near Bamenda pool services to improve access to inputs and buyers. Members elect accountable representatives and decide through the agreed democratic process. Their purpose and governance distinguish the cooperative from a firm controlled only by the largest ordinary-share holding. Neither democratic ownership nor shared marketing guarantees a favourable harvest or price.",
 "Identify the members, the common service and the decision rule. Distinguish capital contribution, voting and benefit allocation: they need not use the same basis. Read the ballot conditions before counting votes and do not invent proxy rules.",[
 F("common member need","a shared economic, social or cultural purpose addressed by the enterprise","Growers jointly seek access to useful services.","A cooperative exists only to maximise an outside investor’s control.","The enterprise’s common member purpose is central to its identity."),
 F("voluntary membership","joining freely while accepting the relevant responsibilities","Eligible users choose to join and accept the rules.","Cooperative membership must always be imposed by force.","Voluntary association differs from compulsory enrolment."),
 F("democratic control","members exercising accountable decision-making power","In the primary model, members elect representatives and each has an equal vote.","The largest contribution automatically buys all decision power in the primary model.","Equal member voting is distinct from voting strictly by capital held."),
 F("member responsibility","the obligations involved in participation and use of the enterprise","Members follow agreed duties rather than only claiming benefits.","Joining a cooperative removes every duty to the enterprise.","Participation includes responsibilities alongside access to benefits.")],votes,prereq=[5])

surplus=number(9,"surplus","A fictional cooperative allocates {total} CFA francs for patronage-based distribution. The stated member’s eligible purchases are {member} out of all eligible purchases of {allp} CFA francs. Using purchases alone as the agreed basis, calculate the member’s allocation.","total*member/allp",
 {"units":{"int":[5,20]},"memberUnits":{"int":[1,4]},"rate":{"int":[2,8]},"allp":{"expr":"units*1000"},"member":{"expr":"memberUnits*1000"},"total":{"expr":"units*rate*100"}},
 ["Use the member’s eligible transactions as a fraction of all eligible transactions.","{total} × {member}/{allp} = {answer} CFA francs."],[("all","total","That is the entire distribution, not one member’s fraction."),("purchases","member","That is transaction value, not the resulting surplus allocation.","member != total*member/allp")])
lesson(9,"Cooperatives: types, surplus and limitations","identify the service supplied by different cooperatives and calculate an allocation under an explicit rule",
 "member ownership and democratic control","public ownership and comparison of enterprise objectives",
 "Producer cooperatives can support members’ production or marketing; consumer cooperatives serve purchasing needs; credit cooperatives concern members’ financial services subject to applicable rules. Benefits may include pooled purchasing, shared facilities and bargaining, while finance, management or participation problems remain possible. A surplus may support reserves, development or members under valid rules. One member, one vote does not mean every payment must be shared equally. A patronage allocation can reflect eligible transactions, as the exercise explicitly states.",
 "A fictional consumer cooperative buys goods for members; a growers’ cooperative arranges shared marketing. After meeting costs, each must follow its rules about reserves and distributions. The classroom allocation uses only the explicitly distributable amount, not all receipts, and does not promise that any real cooperative will pay a return.",
 "Name the service before naming the type. Separate voting equality from transaction-based allocation. Deduct or reserve amounts only when specified; do not distribute a reserve already excluded from the distributable total. Explain a benefit alongside the condition needed to achieve it.",[
 F("producer cooperative","a cooperative serving members’ production or marketing needs","Growers share facilities to prepare produce for buyers.","All cooperatives provide exactly the same service.","The service supplied helps distinguish types of cooperative."),
 F("consumer cooperative","a cooperative serving members’ purchasing needs","Members organise the supply of goods they use.","A consumer cooperative is defined only by selling farm output for growers.","Its focus is members’ consumption or purchasing needs."),
 F("patronage allocation","a distribution based on members’ eligible transactions under an agreed rule","The model allocates a specified surplus using eligible purchases.","Equal votes always require identical patronage payments.","Voting and payment allocation can use different principles."),
 F("management limitation","an organisational problem that can reduce a cooperative’s benefits","Poor records and weak participation make shared marketing harder.","Cooperative status automatically removes every management problem.","The benefits depend on effective records, participation and management.")],surplus,prereq=[8])

gap=number(10,"gap","In a fictional public-service enterprise, service costs are {cost} CFA francs and fee revenue {fees}. For this simplified record, calculate the funding gap before any subsidy or other finance.","cost-fees",
 {"a":{"int":[10,20]},"b":{"int":[3,8]},"cost":{"expr":"a*1000"},"fees":{"expr":"b*1000"}},["Gap = stated costs minus stated fee revenue.","{cost} − {fees} = {answer} CFA francs."],[("sum","cost+fees","Fee income covers part of costs; it does not add to the gap."),("income","fees","That is fee revenue, not the uncovered amount.")])
lesson(10,"Public enterprises","distinguish government ownership from regulation and assess commercial and service objectives",
 "private and cooperative ownership","nationalisation and privatisation as changes in ownership",
 "A public enterprise is owned or controlled by public authorities under its particular arrangement. It may pursue commercial returns, public-service access or strategic objectives. Public ownership differs from regulating a privately owned firm and from selling shares to private members of the public. A mixed-ownership enterprise combines public and private interests. Public enterprises still use scarce resources and require accountability. Neither public ownership nor a service objective proves efficiency, inefficiency or a need for permanent subsidy in every case.",
 "An invented local transport enterprise charges fares while serving a route valued for access. A classroom record compares fee income with service costs, without identifying a real operator or current fare. Learners can discuss who meets any gap and whether the service goal is clearly defined. A regulated private operator would represent a different ownership arrangement.",
 "Separate ownership, control, regulation and objectives. Assess service outcomes and resource costs together. A subsidy can fund a specified obligation but does not make its resource cost disappear. Do not infer the ownership of a real Cameroonian enterprise from its familiar name.",[
 F("public ownership","an enterprise interest held by public authorities","A fictional authority owns the transport enterprise.","Any firm regulated by government is government-owned.","Rules can constrain a private firm without transferring its ownership."),
 F("public-service objective","a purpose such as access or continuity beyond a simple profit target","The enterprise is asked to maintain access on a specified route.","A service objective means no scarce resources are used.","A socially valued service still requires labour, materials and funds."),
 F("mixed ownership","public and private interests sharing ownership in the stated arrangement","The example gives both an authority and private investors ownership interests.","Mixed ownership is identical to a completely private enterprise.","The presence of a public ownership interest makes this arrangement different."),
 F("accountability","responsibility to explain decisions, resources and outcomes","The enterprise reports both service delivery and its costs.","Public ownership removes every need to report performance.","Scarce public resources and service responsibilities require scrutiny.")],gap,prereq=[4,9])

budget=number(11,"budget","A fictional classroom plan has {funds} CFA francs of confirmed funds. Planned equipment costs {equipment} and initial working funds {working}. How much remains after both allocations?","funds-equipment-working",
 {"a":{"int":[15,25]},"b":{"int":[4,7]},"c":{"int":[2,5]},"funds":{"expr":"a*1000"},"equipment":{"expr":"b*1000"},"working":{"expr":"c*1000"}},
 ["Total planned allocation = {equipment} + {working} = {equipment+working}.","Remainder = {funds} − {equipment+working} = {answer} CFA francs."],[("one","funds-equipment","You omitted the initial working-fund allocation."),("sum","funds+equipment+working","Costs and allocations reduce the remaining budget, not increase it.")])
lesson(11,"Guided preparation: a business plan for a locality","justify a fictional business choice using demand, resources, ownership and a simple budget",
 "ownership comparisons, financing and enterprise objectives","scale, growth and location decisions that test the plan further",
 "This classroom project prepares a reasoned plan rather than registering or launching a real business. Identify a possible local need, the intended customers, resources, proposed ownership and likely costs. A need is not automatically paying demand: customers must be willing and able to buy at a viable price. Separate initial equipment from funds needed for everyday operations. A plan should identify what is known, assumed and still to be checked. Learners do not have to spend money or disclose household finances.",
 "An imaginary Buea repair service proposes a small work space, tools and funds for materials. The group explains why its service might be useful, how ownership would work and what evidence would test demand. It lists appropriate permissions as matters to verify, without inventing a current filing checklist or suggesting that classroom approval is legal authorisation.",
 "Write a short exercise-book plan: proposed service; customers and evidence needed; resources; ownership choice with a limitation; labelled budget; risks and checks. Use fictional figures. Review whether funds cover both setup and initial operations, then explain one uncertainty the arithmetic cannot settle.",[
 F("demand evidence","information about willingness and ability to buy the proposed service","The group asks what would show that customers could actually pay.","A pupil’s wish alone proves paying demand from everyone nearby.","Demand needs evidence about potential customers, not just the proposer’s enthusiasm."),
 F("fixed equipment","durable resources used repeatedly in the activity","The plan identifies tools used across many repairs.","Every purchase of a durable tool is a daily wage payment.","Equipment and recurring operating payments are different categories."),
 F("working funds","resources available to support everyday operations","The plan allows funds for materials and routine outgoings.","Buying equipment removes the need for any operating funds.","A business can have tools yet lack funds to meet current expenses."),
 F("feasibility check","testing whether a proposal fits its constraints and evidence","The group checks funds, skills, demand and matters needing authorisation.","A positive budget remainder guarantees the entire business will succeed.","A budget checks one constraint; demand, capability and other conditions still matter.")],budget,prereq=[5,6,9,10])

stateShare=number(12,"public-share","In an invented enterprise with {total} identical voting shares, public authorities acquire {state} shares. What percentage of these shares do they hold?","100*state/total",
 {"total":1000,"a":{"int":[51,90]},"state":{"expr":"a*10"}},["Public share percentage = public shares / total shares × {100}.","{state}/{total} × {100} = {answer}%."],[("private","100*(total-state)/total","That is the remaining private share, not the public holding."),("fraction","state/total","That is the fraction; multiply by one hundred for a percentage.")],unit="%")
lesson(12,"Nationalisation of industries","explain an ownership transfer to the state and assess possible purposes and costs",
 "public, private and mixed ownership","privatisation as a movement in the opposite ownership direction",
 "Nationalisation transfers privately owned enterprise assets or interests into public ownership through the applicable process. Possible aims include strategic control, service access or coordination. It differs from regulating a private business, subsidising it or merely changing its managers. A transfer may involve acquisition costs and continuing financing obligations. The name of the policy does not establish its outcome: efficiency, access and accountability depend on implementation and circumstances. This lesson uses hypothetical transfers, not an assertion about a current Cameroonian policy or compensation rule.",
 "A fictional authority acquires ownership interests in an existing private enterprise to pursue a stated service goal. The enterprise still requires workers, maintenance and oversight. A separate example in which the authority sets quality rules but buys no interest is regulation, not nationalisation. A partial holding must be described accurately rather than called complete ownership.",
 "Identify what ownership changed, who paid or financed it in the model and what goal was stated. Evaluate outcomes with evidence instead of assuming the goal has already been achieved. An ownership percentage alone does not reveal every legal control right in a real enterprise.",[
 F("nationalisation","a transfer from private to public enterprise ownership","The fictional authority acquires an existing private enterprise interest.","Any government quality rule is automatically nationalisation.","A regulation need not transfer ownership."),
 F("strategic-control aim","an intention to direct an activity considered important by public authorities","The stated policy seeks more public direction over a key service.","Stating a strategic aim proves every outcome will be beneficial.","An objective must be distinguished from evidence of results."),
 F("acquisition cost","resources required to obtain ownership in the stated transfer","The fictional purchase uses public funds that have alternative uses.","An ownership purchase can never carry an opportunity cost.","Funds used to acquire an interest are not simultaneously available for every alternative."),
 F("implementation risk","the possibility that the chosen ownership policy fails to achieve its aims","Weak oversight can undermine the proposed service improvement.","Changing ownership automatically removes every management problem.","Management, incentives and accountability still affect performance.")],stateShare,prereq=[10])

privateShare=number(13,"private-share","A fictional state-held block contains {total} identical shares. Under the stated sale, {sold} shares move to private ownership. What percentage of that original block remains publicly held?","100*(total-sold)/total",
 {"total":1000,"a":{"int":[10,80]},"sold":{"expr":"a*10"}},["Public shares remaining = {total} − {sold} = {total-sold}.","As a percentage of the original block: {total-sold}/{total} × {100} = {answer}%."],[("sold","100*sold/total","That is the sold private portion, not the public remainder.","sold*2 != total"),("count","total-sold","That is the share count, not a percentage of the original block.")],unit="%")
lesson(13,"Privatisation of industries","distinguish privatisation from liberalisation or commercialisation and evaluate competing consequences",
 "nationalisation and mixed ownership","judging scale and growth without assuming ownership alone settles performance",
 "Privatisation moves enterprise ownership from public to private interests, wholly or partly in the stated arrangement. It is distinct from liberalisation, which opens or changes competition rules, and commercialisation, which changes how an enterprise operates without necessarily selling it. Possible aims include finance, incentives or efficiency; concerns include access, market power, sale value and employment adjustment. Ownership change alone guarantees none of these outcomes. The lessons compare hypothetical policies rather than recommend a real transaction.",
 "An invented authority sells part of an enterprise’s shares, retaining a public stake. Another authority keeps ownership but asks its enterprise to cover more costs through charges. These are different changes. A market can remain concentrated after an ownership sale, so describing it as private does not establish effective competition or low prices.",
 "State the direction and extent of ownership change. Ask about sale proceeds, continuing obligations, access and competition. Separate a short-term receipt from recurring profit or long-term service outcomes. Read whether a percentage refers to the whole enterprise or only a block of shares.",[
 F("privatisation","a transfer from public to private ownership in the stated enterprise arrangement","Private investors buy part of a publicly held enterprise.","Privatisation always means the state acquires private shares.","That is the opposite ownership direction."),
 F("liberalisation","opening or changing market-entry and competition rules","Rules allow additional suppliers without selling the existing public enterprise.","Allowing entry must always sell the existing enterprise.","Competition rules and ownership are separate dimensions."),
 F("commercialisation","operating on a more commercial basis without necessarily transferring ownership","A publicly owned service changes charging and cost practices while ownership stays public.","Every change in charges proves that ownership has been sold.","Operating methods can change while ownership remains unchanged."),
 F("market-power concern","the risk that a dominant seller can limit competition regardless of ownership","The fictional sale leaves one dominant provider.","Private ownership automatically guarantees many competing sellers.","Ownership and the number or strength of competitors are different issues.")],privateShare,prereq=[12])

economy=number(14,"average-saving","For comparable long-run production plans, an invented workshop can make {q} units at total cost {c1}, or {2*q} units at total cost {c2} CFA francs. How much lower is average cost per unit in the larger plan?","c1/q-c2/(2*q)",
 {"q":{"int":[10,40]},"ac":{"int":[300,600]},"save":{"int":[20,100]},"c1":{"expr":"q*ac"},"c2":{"expr":"2*q*(ac-save)"}},
 ["Smaller-plan average cost = {c1}/{q} = {ac}.","Larger-plan average cost = {c2}/{2*q} = {ac-save}.","Saving per unit = {ac} − {ac-save} = {answer} CFA francs."],[("total","c1-c2","Compare average costs, not a difference between total costs."),("large","c2/(2*q)","That is the new average cost, not the decrease in it.")],unit="CFA francs per unit")
lesson(14,"Economies of scale","explain why larger scale can lower long-run average cost and calculate the saving per unit",
 "resources, finance and the cost of supplying output","diseconomies of scale and the limits to expansion",
 "Economies of scale occur when larger output scale lowers long-run average cost as the firm can vary all inputs. Internal economies arise within the firm, such as specialisation, technical indivisibilities or purchasing advantages. External economies can arise from an industry’s expansion, such as specialised suppliers or a relevant skills pool. Total cost can rise while average cost falls. A purchasing discount is useful only if storage, quality and demand make the larger order worthwhile. Size does not guarantee a cost saving.",
 "A fictional Douala workshop compares complete production plans at different scales. Specialist equipment may spread its services over more output, and a larger firm may organise specialised tasks. A cluster of firms can also support a specialist supplier. These are possible mechanisms, not proof that every large firm is efficient or every observed cost fall comes from scale.",
 "Compute average cost as total cost divided by comparable output. State whether the cause is internal to one firm or external through its industry. Separate long-run scale from a short-run change with fixed capacity. Lower cost may enable lower prices, but the actual price also depends on market conditions.",[
 F("internal economy","a scale-related cost advantage arising within the firm","The firm can justify specialised equipment as its scale expands.","Every internal economy comes only from unrelated firms expanding.","The source is within the expanding firm’s own organisation or technology."),
 F("external economy","a benefit linked to expansion of the industry or cluster rather than one firm alone","Industry growth supports a specialised local supplier used by several firms.","External economies mean only the firm’s owner works longer hours.","The benefit arises from the industry environment, not that personal work change."),
 F("average cost","total cost divided by the corresponding output","The workshop compares cost per unit rather than total spending alone.","A higher total cost always proves a higher cost per unit.","Output may rise faster than total cost, reducing average cost."),
 F("purchasing economy","a scale-related opportunity to obtain inputs on lower unit-cost terms","A larger appropriate order may obtain better input terms.","Every bulk purchase must save overall resources regardless of waste.","Storage, spoilage and demand can offset the apparent input-price saving.")],economy,
 worked({},"Compare a smaller long-run plan costing {10000} for {20} units with a larger plan costing {16000} for {40} units.",["Average costs: {10000/20} and {16000/40} CFA francs per unit.","The larger plan saves {10000/20-16000/40} per unit even though its total cost is higher."],"Average cost falls by {10000/20-16000/40} CFA francs per unit."),prereq=[6,11])

diseconomy=number(15,"average-rise","Comparable long-run plans cost {c1} for {q} units and {c2} for {2*q} units, in CFA francs. Calculate the increase in average cost per unit at the larger scale.","c2/(2*q)-c1/q",
 {"q":{"int":[10,40]},"ac":{"int":[200,500]},"rise":{"int":[20,100]},"c1":{"expr":"q*ac"},"c2":{"expr":"2*q*(ac+rise)"}},
 ["Initial average cost = {c1}/{q} = {ac}.","Larger-scale average cost = {c2}/{2*q} = {ac+rise}.","Increase = {answer} CFA francs per unit."],[("total","c2-c1","That is the total-cost increase, not the per-unit change."),("new","ac+rise","That is the larger average cost, not its increase.")],unit="CFA francs per unit")
lesson(15,"Diseconomies of scale","identify scale-related coordination costs and calculate a rise in long-run average cost",
 "economies of scale and average-cost comparisons","evaluating growth instead of assuming bigger is always better",
 "Diseconomies of scale occur when expansion raises long-run average cost. Communication delays, coordination difficulties and weak monitoring can offset benefits of size. External diseconomies can arise as industry expansion creates congestion or pressure on scarce local resources. A temporary input-price shock is not automatically an internal diseconomy caused by a firm’s scale. Economies and diseconomies may operate together; the net cost pattern matters. Higher total spending alone does not establish diseconomies.",
 "An invented processor expands across several departments. Instructions arrive late and incompatible schedules cause rework. In another case, industry-wide congestion delays many firms. The first mechanism arises within the firm, while the second concerns the local industry environment. Neither proves that every large organisation must fail.",
 "Look for an explained link from scale to cost. Compare cost per unit under comparable plans, and distinguish internal from external causes. Then propose a response, such as clearer coordination, while recognising that a remedy also uses resources. Do not confuse long-run scale with diminishing returns to one variable input at fixed capacity.",[
 F("internal diseconomy","a scale-related disadvantage arising within the firm","Additional layers delay instructions and cause rework in the expanded firm.","Higher output always lowers average cost without limit.","Coordination and monitoring costs can offset scale advantages."),
 F("external diseconomy","a disadvantage arising from industry expansion in the surrounding environment","Industry-wide congestion delays deliveries for several firms.","Congestion affecting many firms must be a personal dispute inside one firm.","The stated source lies in the wider industry environment."),
 F("coordination problem","difficulty aligning interdependent tasks and decisions","Departments expand but their production schedules no longer fit.","More departments automatically coordinate without effort.","Interdependent work needs communication and compatible plans."),
 F("average-cost test","comparing cost per unit rather than total expenditure to identify the cost pattern","The pupil divides each plan’s total cost by its own output.","An increase in total cost by itself proves diseconomies.","Output changes too, so the per-unit comparison is required.")],diseconomy,prereq=[14])

growth=number(16,"growth-rate","An invented firm’s comparable annual output rises from {old} to {new} units. Calculate the percentage increase.","100*(new-old)/old",
 {"old":{"pick":[100,200,400]},"rate":{"pick":[10,20,25,50]},"new":{"expr":"old*(100+rate)/100"}},
 ["Increase = {new} − {old} = {new-old} units.","Percentage growth = increase/original × {100} = {answer}%."],[("newbase","100*(new-old)/new","Use the original output as the growth-rate denominator."),("difference","new-old","That is the absolute increase, not necessarily its percentage.","new-old != 100*(new-old)/old")],unit="%")
lesson(16,"Growth of firms: internal growth and measures","distinguish size measures, calculate growth and explain how internal expansion can be financed",
 "scale economies, diseconomies and financing","external growth through mergers and acquisitions",
 "Internal or organic growth expands a firm’s own operations, for example by adding capacity, products or outlets. It can be financed through retained profit, new owners’ funds or borrowing; internal growth does not mean finance must come only from retained earnings. Firm size can be measured by output, employees, assets or sales, which may rank firms differently. Profit is not identical to size. Compare the same measure and period when calculating a growth rate.",
 "A fictional Bamenda bakery adds its own oven and expands production without buying another enterprise. It may employ more workers and produce more units, but the larger output does not guarantee a higher profit if costs also rise. A rise in sales value can reflect price changes rather than an equal rise in physical output.",
 "Name the measure, find the absolute change and divide by the original value for a percentage. State whether a change is organic or comes from combining firms. Assess demand, funds and management capacity before treating an expansion target as feasible.",[
 F("internal growth","expansion through the firm’s own operations rather than combining with another business","The fictional bakery installs its own additional oven.","Internal growth can only be financed by retained profit.","The growth method and the financing source are different classifications."),
 F("retained earnings","profits kept in the business rather than distributed","The owner leaves part of past profits to support equipment needs.","Retained earnings are all sales receipts before costs.","Only profit retained after relevant costs is retained earnings."),
 F("size measure","a specified indicator such as output, employment or assets","A comparison states physical output rather than calling every larger receipt more production.","A sales-value increase proves the same percentage rise in physical output.","Prices as well as quantity can change sales value."),
 F("growth rate","the change relative to the original value, expressed as a percentage","A pupil compares the output increase with the starting output.","A growth percentage always uses the final value as its base.","The original value is the base for the standard percentage increase.")],growth,prereq=[6,14,15])

MERGERS=[("horizontal integration","Two fictional bakeries at the same production stage combine.","They operate at the same stage in the same industry."),("backward vertical integration","A fictional bakery acquires its flour supplier.","The firm moves towards an earlier input-supply stage."),("forward vertical integration","A fictional bakery acquires retail outlets selling its bread.","The firm moves towards distribution and the final customer."),("conglomerate integration","A fictional bakery combines with an unrelated furniture maker.","The combined activities are in unrelated lines in this model.")]
integration=qa(17,"identify",2,[item(case+" Classify the integration.",term,[(other,"That describes a different relationship between the firms’ industries or stages.") for other,c,y in MERGERS if other!=term],why) for term,case,why in MERGERS])
lesson(17,"Growth of firms: mergers, takeovers and integration","classify external growth by the firms’ relationship and evaluate a possible gain against a risk",
 "internal growth and the benefits and limits of scale","why some firms remain small and how growth changes location needs",
 "External growth combines existing businesses through arrangements such as mergers or takeovers. Horizontal integration links firms at the same stage in the same industry. Vertical integration links different stages: backward towards inputs, forward towards customers. Conglomerate integration combines unrelated activities in the classroom model. Merger and takeover describe how businesses combine or control changes, while horizontal and vertical describe their relationship. Potential savings, supply security or diversification must be weighed against integration costs, financing risk and reduced competition.",
 "A fictional bakery buying a flour supplier differs from buying another bakery or a retail outlet. The first can improve coordination with supply, but ownership does not guarantee low input costs. Combining rivals can reduce competition even if the managers also expect savings. None of these examples announces a real acquisition.",
 "Draw the production chain before choosing forward or backward. State both a plausible mechanism of benefit and a possible disadvantage. Do not assume combining firms automatically adds their profits without transition costs or that diversification eliminates every risk.",[
 F("horizontal integration","combining firms at the same stage in the same industry","Two fictional bakeries combine their businesses.","Every combination of any unrelated firms is horizontal.","The firms must share the industry and production stage in this classification."),
 F("backward integration","extending control towards an earlier input-supply stage","The fictional bakery acquires its flour supplier.","Buying an input supplier always moves the firm towards final retail customers.","An input supplier is upstream, so the direction is backward."),
 F("forward integration","extending control towards distribution or final customers","The bakery acquires retail outlets selling its bread.","Buying retail outlets is always backward integration.","Retail lies closer to final customers than the bakery’s production stage."),
 F("conglomerate integration","combining unrelated activities in the stated model","The bakery combines with an unrelated furniture maker.","Diversification guarantees that every business risk disappears.","Different activities can spread exposure but still involve shared finance and management risks.")],integration,prereq=[16])

small=number(18,"small-profit","A fictional specialised repair firm completes {jobs} jobs, receiving {price} CFA francs per job. Total stated costs are {cost}. Calculate accounting profit for this period.","jobs*price-cost",
 {"jobs":{"int":[5,15]},"price":{"pick":[1000,1500,2000]},"unitcost":{"pick":[400,600,800]},"cost":{"expr":"jobs*unitcost"}},
 ["Revenue = {jobs} × {price} = {jobs*price} CFA francs.","Profit = revenue − total stated costs = {answer} CFA francs."],[("revenue","jobs*price","That is revenue before deducting costs."),("one","price-unitcost","That is profit per job, not for all completed jobs.")])
lesson(18,"Small firms: roles, survival and limits","explain why a small firm may survive and calculate profit without confusing small size with failure",
 "growth methods and scale advantages or disadvantages","location choices suited to the activity and its customers",
 "Small firms can survive through specialised demand, personal service, flexibility, local knowledge or a market too limited to support large-scale production. Size depends on the measure and context, not a universal employee cutoff supplied by this lesson. Small firms may also face weak bargaining power, limited finance and dependence on few people or customers. A firm can deliberately remain small and profitable; another can remain small because it cannot obtain resources to expand. Neither case follows from size alone.",
 "An invented Buea repairer serves customers who value adjustments and direct contact. Demand may suit a small workshop rather than mass production. A large competitor may have lower standard-unit costs, while the small firm supplies a different service. These advantages require relevant skills and customers willing to pay, not just a label such as friendly business.",
 "Link each survival explanation to demand or production conditions. Compare revenue and costs before judging profitability. Do not infer that small firms create only benefits or that every owner wants expansion. Distinguish being small from being legally a sole proprietor: a company can also be small.",[
 F("niche demand","a specialised segment of customers with particular requirements","The repairer serves unusual adjustments that mass suppliers do not offer.","Every niche automatically provides unlimited sales.","A specialised segment can be viable but still has a limited market."),
 F("personal service","direct attention suited to an individual customer’s requirements","The owner discusses a customer’s particular repair needs.","Personal attention guarantees profitability regardless of costs.","Service can attract demand, but costs and willingness to pay still matter."),
 F("flexibility","ability to adjust activities relatively quickly in the stated setting","The small workshop changes its service mix as local needs change.","Small firms can change any activity without resources or skills.","Adaptation still faces capacity, skill and finance constraints."),
 F("resource limitation","a shortage restricting what the firm can do or how it can expand","The workshop cannot finance suitable additional equipment.","Small size always proves the owner freely rejected all growth.","Some firms remain small because constraints restrict feasible choices.")],small,prereq=[15,16,17])

transport=number(19,"transport","A fictional processor compares input transport. Site A costs {ain} CFA francs per load for {loads} loads; Site B costs {bin} per load for the same loads. How much lower is this input-transport cost at A?","loads*(bin-ain)",
 {"loads":{"int":[4,15]},"ain":{"pick":[100,200,300]},"extra":{"pick":[100,200,400]},"bin":{"expr":"ain+extra"}},
 ["A input-transport cost = {loads} × {ain} = {loads*ain}.","B input-transport cost = {loads} × {bin} = {loads*bin}.","B minus A = {answer} CFA francs; other location factors remain to be compared."],[("one","bin-ain","That is the saving per load; multiply by the number of loads."),("sum","loads*(ain+bin)","Additions do not give the cost difference between sites.")])
lesson(19,"Location of firms: production and market factors","explain a location factor’s effect on an activity and calculate a stated transport-cost difference",
 "firm objectives, scale and specialised demand","comparing complete sites and the benefits or costs of industrial clusters",
 "Location is the choice of where an activity operates. Relevant factors include inputs, customers, labour skills, power, water, transport, land and communication. Their importance depends on the activity. A bulky or perishable input can favour proximity to supply; customer contact may favour access to buyers. Cheap land alone does not make a site viable if essential services fail. A firm can face conflicting factors, so a location explanation must connect the factor to costs, revenue or feasibility.",
 "A fictional processor near Buea compares input transport with access to customers. An urban repair service may value nearby clients, while an activity dependent on a specific resource faces a different constraint. No actual route, site, wage, tariff or safety condition is asserted; the figures supplied are invented comparisons.",
 "Name the product and its production needs first. Calculate the relevant transport component with consistent units, then ask what is missing. A saving on inputs may be offset by a larger outward-delivery cost. Explain why the same factor can matter differently to different activities.",[
 F("input access","proximity or connection to the resources an activity needs","A bulky-input processor compares the cost of bringing materials to each site.","Every business must locate beside the same raw material.","Activities require different inputs, so their location priorities differ."),
 F("market access","the ability to reach customers effectively","A repair service considers how easily clients can reach it.","Nearness to customers cannot affect demand or delivery costs.","Accessibility can affect both service demand and distribution costs."),
 F("labour suitability","availability of the skills and workforce needed for the activity","The firm checks relevant technical skills rather than headcount alone.","Any available worker automatically has every specialist skill.","The type and capability of labour matter as well as its number."),
 F("infrastructure reliability","dependability of essential services and connections","A processor checks power and water continuity before accepting cheap land.","A low land price proves essential services are adequate.","A site can be cheap yet fail an essential operating requirement.")],transport,prereq=[11,18])

site=number(20,"site-cost","Invented monthly cost record: Site A has rent {arent}, input transport {ain} and outward delivery {aout}; Site B’s complete comparable total is {btotal}, all in CFA francs. How much lower is A’s stated total than B’s?","btotal-(arent+ain+aout)",
 {"arent":{"pick":[2000,3000,4000]},"ain":{"pick":[1000,1500,2000]},"aout":{"pick":[500,1000,1500]},"saving":{"pick":[500,1000,2000]},"btotal":{"expr":"arent+ain+aout+saving"}},
 ["A total = {arent} + {ain} + {aout} = {arent+ain+aout}.","B minus A = {btotal} − {arent+ain+aout} = {answer} CFA francs.","This compares only the stated costs; check service reliability and other constraints separately."],[("rentonly","btotal-arent","That omits A’s input and outward transport costs."),("atotal","arent+ain+aout","That is A’s total, not its saving relative to B.")])
lesson(20,"Location of firms: site choice, clusters and policy","compare complete site evidence and distinguish firm location from industrial concentration",
 "input, market, labour and infrastructure factors","later production, cost and market analysis using evidence rather than one-factor rules",
 "Location chooses a site for an activity; localisation describes concentration of related activities in an area. A cluster may support skills, suppliers and shared services, but congestion, competition for resources or environmental pressures can offset benefits. Public authorities may influence location through infrastructure or incentives. An incentive is one factor, not proof of an efficient or suitable site. Compare complete relevant costs and essential constraints; some social and environmental effects cannot be reduced to the few amounts in a classroom table.",
 "A fictional repair cluster near Bamenda offers access to suppliers and trained workers, but its crowded access road may delay deliveries. A proposed alternative has lower rent yet less reliable services. The case asks pupils to compare stated evidence and identify missing facts, without recommending a real place or claiming a current government incentive.",
 "Use a comparison sheet: rent, inward transport, outward delivery, essential services, workforce and effects on others. Sum only comparable monetary items and avoid counting the same cost twice. State a provisional choice with a reason and a limitation. A cheapest stated total is not enough if the site fails an essential requirement.",[
 F("localisation","concentration of related industrial activities in an area","Several related workshops operate near common specialist suppliers.","Localisation means only one firm choosing any isolated site.","The term concerns a concentration of activities, not just an individual location."),
 F("cluster benefit","an advantage from nearby related firms, skills or suppliers","Shared access to a specialist input service benefits several workshops.","Clustering always has advantages and never congestion costs.","Benefits must be weighed against crowding and competing resource demands."),
 F("location incentive","a policy measure intended to attract or support activity in a place","A hypothetical authority offers support that changes a site’s stated costs.","An incentive alone proves every site condition is suitable.","Service, demand and operating constraints remain even if one cost is reduced."),
 F("complete site comparison","weighing relevant costs and constraints rather than a single attractive feature","The pupil compares transport and reliability as well as rent.","The lowest rent is always the lowest total cost and best feasible site.","Other costs and essential conditions can overturn a rent-only ranking.")],site,
 worked({},"A fictional site has rent {3000}, input transport {2000} and delivery {1000}; the alternative’s comparable total is {7500} CFA francs. Compare the stated totals.",["First total = {3000}+{2000}+{1000} = {3000+2000+1000}.","Saving = {7500}−{3000+2000+1000} = {7500-3000-2000-1000}.","Check missing costs and essential services before deciding."],"The first site costs {7500-3000-2000-1000} CFA francs less on the stated items."),prereq=[14,15,19])

